1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:00,960
Hey, everyone.

2
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:09,040
Imagine you've poured time, money and effort into a deal, but just as you're locked in, the other side changes the terms.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,680
And now you can't afford to walk away.

4
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:15,680
You've just fallen to a classic trap, the post investment holdup.

5
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:18,560
It starts with sunk cost and ends with regret.

6
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:22,480
To avoid it, you have to look ahead and reason back.

7
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,400
Sunk costs are past investments you can't recover.

8
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:29,920
Time, money, labor.  It's all gone.  The mistake?

9
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,280
Letting those costs dictate your future decisions.

10
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:37,040
Rational thinking says, ignore them.  They're gone.

11
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:41,120
Good strategy.  Ask what are the next cost and benefits?

12
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:43,360
Not what have I already spent?

13
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:51,200
Post investment holdup happens when you commit big upfront and your partner waits until you're stuck to change the deal.

14
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:53,120
Maybe they raise prices.

15
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:57,040
Maybe they threaten to walk away unless you renegotiate.

16
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:02,240
You can't walk away because you've already built the factory, coded the software, and you've gone all in.

17
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,480
We assume the deal won't change.

18
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:07,760
We assume trust and past agreements will hold.

19
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,360
But the moment your investment is sunk, the power shifts.

20
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:14,000
They hold the leverage, you hold the risk.

21
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:16,000
It sounds simple.

22
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:17,440
Look ahead and reason back.

23
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:19,040
But it's hard in practice.

24
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:24,720
Ask yourself, what incentives will my partner have after I commit?

25
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:27,280
What could they do that hurts me?

26
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:30,800
Once I'm locked in, would I still do the deal?

27
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:32,160
Starting from scratch.

28
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:35,840
Smart partners build in protection before they commit.

29
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:37,440
Here's how.

30
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:40,240
Detail contracts.

31
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:44,320
Define pricing, timelines and dispute resolution before you invest.

32
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:48,840
A strong contract locks in the terms before power shifts.

33
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:52,360
Cap pricing to avoid surprise markups.

34
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:55,160
Set timelines with penalties for delays.

35
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:58,360
Include clear paths for resolving disputes.

36
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:00,680
Exchange of hostages.

37
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:07,160
Each side puts up something valuable, something they lose if they act opportunistically.

38
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:12,680
Escrow funds, joint intellectual property and mutually valuable deliverables.

39
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:17,240
Progressive investment.  Don't commit everything at once.

40
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:20,520
Structure deals and stages tied to progress.

41
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:23,320
Prototype pilot, full scale.

42
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:28,440
Each stage unlocks the next, reducing total exposure.

43
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:31,000
And finally, build exit options.

44
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,920
Keep a plan B in your back pocket.

45
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:35,080
Write a termination costs.

46
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:36,840
Vet background partners.

47
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:40,760
Early use modular systems that can be reused elsewhere.

48
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,840
The goal isn't to avoid investment, it's to avoid regret.

49
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:47,280
Sunk costs blur your judgment.

50
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:49,960
Post investment holdup kills your leverage.

51
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:53,400
So before you commit, look ahead and reason back.

52
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:54,840
Protect yourself.

53
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:57,960
Don't build something you can't walk away from.

54
00:02:58,520 --> 00:02:59,520
And that's all we have.

55
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:00,520
Hope you've enjoyed it.